And I think especially like with Subclub, you guys focus more on subscriptions obviously and mobile apps.

39
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:56,000
We're focused more with our podcast on learning from the world's fastest growing companies and trying to understand how they operationalize growth, how they build growth.

40
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:58,480
But there's a lot of things that are connected between those.

41
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:01,120
So it's really cool to be invited on to talk to you today.

42
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:01,760
Yeah.

43
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:04,080
And that's actually where I wanted to kick things off.

44
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:13,600
So, Ethan, you probably have a little more subscription app specific experience having worked at Teltek, which made RoboKiller and then got acquired by IAC.

45
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:16,800
And then, you know, you were kind of in IAC for a bit.

46
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:22,480
And then, Sean, you've just worked with so many amazing companies, including subscription app companies.

47
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:29,520
But on your breakout growth website, you have this really great downloadable PDF about the principles of sustainable growth.

48
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:32,560
And it's really cool how so many of the principles.

49
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:45,760
of growth really do apply fairly universally, but then I'd love to kind of walk through these principles of sustainable growth maybe with just a little bit more bent towards our audience of subscription apps.

50
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:59,840
So let's go through each of those and I'd love to just kind of hear some high level thoughts on like what those mean to you and then maybe I'll pester you with questions on how maybe it more even directly applies or doesn't apply to subscription apps.

51
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:09,200
So the first one on the list is dial in the must have most tab experience?

52
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:11,280
Why is that a principle for growth?

53
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:25,280
Yeah, so essentially, you know, I think it's pretty well accepted these days, at least in the startup world, that without product market fit, you really can't drive sustainable growth.

54
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:36,000
And what that really means is that essentially, if people try your product and they don't keep using your product, eventually, you're going to be losing people faster than you're bringing people in.

55
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:42,560
And so, it's really retention at the core of this, but then, then it's really really what drives retention.

56
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:50,320
And the thing that drives retention is that when someone does try your product, they say, wow, I really need this.

57
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:52,720
And it's a must have for me.

58
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:55,200
It solves a really critical need that I have.

59
00:05:55,680 --> 00:06:04,560
And so that ultimately becomes the foundation of growth, whether you're talking about a subscription business or really any type of business.

60
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:13,920
If it doesn't perform the job that it's being hired to do, then people are not going to they're not going to keep using it.

61
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:26,400
And so obviously there are some products that are not necessarily long term use cases, but for long term use cases products that's best represented in retention.

62
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:42,480
And so what the must have really is about is getting into the qualitative of what type of experience does the product need to deliver to the right people who have the right need in order to make it a must have.

63
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:47,360
And so that's that can be a super long process of dialing that in.

64
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:48,800
And I'll show you.

65
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:50,640
And a lot of companies never get that right.

66
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:52,080
And as a result, go out of business.

67
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:57,120
But that's that we definitely thought that was the most critical of all of the principles.

68
00:06:57,200 --> 00:06:58,480
So that's why we started with that.

69
00:06:58,960 --> 00:06:59,040
Yeah.

70
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:07,360
I mean, and Ethan, if you want to chime in, and I'm just curious for both of you though, like, what does that dialing in process look like?

71
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:11,360
So, you know, I'm assuming this must have experience.

72
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:19,760
I think that, you know, a lot of apps get built where, where they think it's a must have or it's a must have for them, but it's not necessarily a broader market.

73
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:30,880
And, and, you know, a lot of folksks try the if they build it, if I build it, they will come approach of like they, you know, come up with their own problem, but it's not a real problem in the market.

74
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:32,480
So it's not really a must have.

75
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:49,840
So how do you go from I think this is a must have to like actually kind of like proving out the market and understanding what that real, that Yeah, let me let me just Yeah, just because that's I've I've basically I put out a process for that that now is pretty widely used.

76
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:53,920
And the idea, just as you said, I think it's a must have.

77
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:56,480
I think a lot of people kind of get into that mode.

78
00:07:56,800 --> 00:08:17,680
So the only way to know if it's a must have is to get it in the hands of potential customers and ultimately ask them how they would feel if they if they could no longer use it, if you if you stopped if you cut them off from the product, would they would they even care?

79
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:22,080
If they wouldn't care, then, um, then your product's not a must have.

80
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:27,920
And even if they say, I'd be somewhat disappointed, then your product's still a nice to have.

81
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:36,960
And so the way that I've honed into that over., you know, more than a decade now is with a single question where I ask that, how would you feel if you could no longer use this product?

82
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:42,000
And I give them the choice very disappointed, somewhat disappointed, not disappointed.

83
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:50,080
And those who answer very disappointed are giving me that signal that they consider the product it must have.

84
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:53,040
And then how do you actually dial it in?

85
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:55,760
You learn everything you can about those people.

86
00:08:56,240 --> 00:08:57,360
What problem were they solving?

87
00:08:57,600 --> 00:08:58,800
How were they solving it before?

88
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:01,280
What is their profile?

89
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:12,400
You know, and so you can, you can really filter survey results to, you know, the most basic of filters might be, let me just see what the men say.

90
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:13,920
Let me just see what the women say.

91
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:26,960
And, you know, if it's a product that's that's more of a feminine needs product, then it's going to not be really important for most men and it's going to be probably really critical for women or vice versa.

92
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:31,760
And so, but obviously there's a lot of different ways you can, you can segment the data.

93
00:09:33,280 --> 00:09:47,120
And so what you're really trying to do is find that white hot group of must have need there and then build a growth engine to acquire more of those people and bring them into the right experience.

94
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:50,480
So Ethan, is there anything you would add on top of what I discovered there?

95
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:58,880
Well, just, you know, I think with that question, I think what and Sean actually led a workshop for us at Tel Tech years ago.

96
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:09,040
And we actually really started to understand that question in the context of other questions and how to really use that to operationalize our growth.

97
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:14,480
So we would actually ask that question as part of a survey every week week while we were growing RoboKiller.

98
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:34,240
With every change, we were trying to look at that and really understand the dynamics of what was driving value for end users, how they were, because I think another thing that Sean and I have talked about extensively is that companies fail because they don't find product market fit all the time, but they also fail to grow because they don't understand their product market fit.

99
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:43,200
So I think using that as a tool, as opposed to just like when we talk about these as principles, what Sean and I always want to do is try to make them actionable.

100
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:46,960
And I think that survey, and I think Sean, you put that in the survey.

101
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:53,120
I want to make sure I get the URL right, but I think it's pmsurvey dot com where you actually have that sample survey.

102
00:10:54,160 --> 00:10:58,320
It's really a good tool that teams can use in mobile and subscription business.

103
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:06,480
I think it's great, but I think really any business to understand where they are on that sort of product market fit kind of continuum, because I don't think it's a gate that you get to and stop.

104
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:09,680
I think it's a gate that you pass through and continue to dial in over time.

105
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:10,400
Yeah.

106
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:20,960
So, and then at Teltek, when you were kind of operationalizing this, would you like to prototype a new feature and then have them do it?

107
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:22,160
for a little bit?

108
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:26,720
Or would you kind of roll out like fully flesh features and then ask those questions?

109
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:28,720
Would you do this in beta?

110
00:11:28,880 --> 00:11:30,160
Would you do it in production?

111
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:37,520
Like, what was the like operational dialing in process look like for a for a subscription app specifically?

112
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:48,000
Well, with RoboKiller, like, you know, we co invented RoboKiller and then grew it from zero to what it became, you know, in eventually the eventually the acquisition by IC.

113
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:53,200
So I think we were moving so fast that it was a little bit of a combination of both.

114
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:59,280
Some of the things we just hadn't finished yet, I think, as we were building it out and we were learning along the way.

115
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:04,640
One of our, you know, our core features, our answer bots was sort of a response to like, well, what do we do here?

116
00:12:04,880 --> 00:12:05,680
What do we do next?

117
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:10,960
And it turned out to be life changing for us, the answer to that.

118
00:12:12,080 --> 00:12:15,360
But I don't think there's a clear cut answer for that for every business.

119
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:25,600
I think it's going to depend on your sort of where you are with our other products, such as tape call and trap call, which were more seasoned at the time and they, you know, they had a following and audience.

120
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:28,000
I think it was a little bit more systematic in that approach.

121
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:28,720
Right.

122
00:12:29,440 --> 00:12:30,640
Yeah, that's really interesting.

123
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:58,800
It's early on in the, in trying to find product market fit, it is really tough to find that balance of, you know, would this feature be, and would this app even, would this idea, you know, kind of be more valuable once it's fully fleshed out or, you know, what, what fidelity of kind of MVP do I need to build to actually get a real answer from users as to whether or not it really is a must have.

124
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:01,680
Yeah, I know Ethan's got, it looked like you were about to jump in.

125
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:13,520
Let me just say one piece and then I'll do it short, but I think, I think there's this belief that, um, you know, if I, if I put more stuff in it, it's going to be more valuable to people.

126
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:22,720
And so, a lot of times, someone feels like, oh, my vision has to be kind of fleshed out before I can really get that feedback on, on the must have.

127
00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:30,000
And, um, you know, one of the things I, I, I learned from, uh, I have a course called, uh, go practice.

128
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:35,040
IO where, where we really talk a lot about this, how, how to get to product market fit.

129
00:13:35,360 --> 00:13:36,720
And my co creator on the course.

130
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:42,080
is a former data scientist from Facebook, but has a lot of early stage startup experience as well.

131
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:57,360
And, uh, he he essentially shared a quote that I think is a really like profound one that I wish I had understood earlier, um, in my quest for product market fit, um, is that it's, it's really hard to iterate something that's already complex.

132
00:13:57,840 --> 00:14:01,280
It's much easier to iterate something that's, that's simple.

133
00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:08,160
And, and, and, and the fact that in most cases, product market fit requires iteration.

134
00:14:08,480 --> 00:14:14,560
So the more that it has jammed into it, the harder you're going to be able to find that line.

135
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:47,600
So it's not to say that complex products can't be successful, but complex products that are that start as simple products that solve a really basic need in an elegant and good way, once you have achieved that must have status on that, on that narrow set of functionality, then you can start to say, okay, now how do I work towards my vision where I make this more defensible by building in that richness and, and, you know, work with that feedback loop of customers to build something that's that's really tight around that core benefit.

136
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:56,080
So I don't know, Ethan, if what you were going to say sort of supports that assertion or maybe, maybe takes it in a different direction.

137
00:14:56,320 --> 00:14:57,120
No, I agree.

138
00:14:57,280 --> 00:15:03,440
I think you're talking about what job are you trying to accomplish, what's the job to be done for that product and really focusing on that.

139
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:04,480
And I agree with that.

140
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:07,920
I think I was just going back to a Tel Tech, one of the challenges.

141
00:15:08,080 --> 00:15:13,360
And I think this is where it's very hard to just look at, you know, with a broad brush at all apps.

142
00:15:13,920 --> 00:15:18,560
But because we were a Tel, we were using telecom systems, systems that were external to our product.

143
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,920
It could be very hard to make an MVP of a product where, you know, to test those things.

144
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:31,120
So sometimes you have to be really creative in how you ask questions to potential users, but ultimately you have to make some big bets.

145
00:15:32,080 --> 00:15:45,520
But I do think if the big bets start with what does our product market fit, why users love what we do and what do they actually get value out of, instead of just jumping to some sort of those tactical solutions, you're much more likely to find features that matter.

146
00:15:46,320 --> 00:15:49,920
Because certainly I'm guilty of building things that no one wanted.

147
00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:52,000
We've all been there.

148
00:15:52,800 --> 00:16:03,760
I just want to add one more thing too that I think is kind of interesting here that I would say my time at Dropbox right out of the gate, we had pretty strong product market fit.

149
00:16:04,560 --> 00:16:12,160
But what I realized pretty quickly was that the customers who love the product the most were using it in all the ways it could be used.

150
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:15,680
So they're synchronizing their data across devices.

151
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,960
They're sharing files to people who don't even have Dropbox.

152
00:16:19,440 --> 00:16:22,880
They're setting up collaborative folders with other people with Dropboxes.

153
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:25,920
There's just a lot of different things you could do with the product.

154
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:38,160
And so the initial thought is, okay, for new people, I'm going to promote all of these things you can do with the product and and really get them to that that must have experience of being able to do all of these things.

155
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:44,480
But I but I found that actually that didn't work very well because that complexity scared people.

156
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:51,360
They could see themselves having one need, but they couldn't necessarily visualize the need for all of those things.

157
00:16:51,680 --> 00:17:15,040
So what we had to do was figure out what was the right narrow use case that we could, you know, and functionality that supported that use case that we could onboard people into once we converted them on that use case, then spoon feed the rest of the product to them kind of one at a time until they until they had that full experience within the product.

158
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:24,640
But even once we converted them on a single use case, like a file share, so if someone shared a file with them, that's what they looked at the product as.

159
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:33,840
And so once we converted them to the file sharing use case, then you know, you can you can actually set up a full collaborative folder.

160
00:17:33,920 --> 00:17:38,880
So what's what's the next ste most contextually relevant thing to that first use case?

161
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:45,360
And then eventually, you know, you can actually just use this for yourself to synchronize your data across all your devices.

162
00:17:46,240 --> 00:17:53,120
And then we knew once we got people doing all those things, when we asked that question, how would you feel if you could no longer use Dropbox?

163
00:17:53,440 --> 00:18:00,640
If they were using everything, it was like they got up to like eighty percent of the people saying they would be very disappointed without the product.

164
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:05,920
But any one of those things, typically, it was, you know, between thirty and forty percent.

165
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:08,560
And so, um, but the fact is.

166
00:18:09,120 --> 00:18:20,720
that if you can get to around forty percent, that's the benchmark that I've seen that it becomes good enough that you can generally build a good sustainable business on it.

167
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:21,280
Yeah.

168
00:18:21,520 --> 00:18:34,640
And I was going to ask this later, but since we're going so deep into product market fit, we talked previously about in our prep call about how understanding your product market fit is the key to operationalizing it.

169
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:50,480
And so you were kind of like dancing around that a little bit just then kind of talking about laying on the different features and not overwhelming people, but there's also this idea that you can you can have product market fit but kind of not fully understand it.

170
00:18:50,720 --> 00:18:53,440
So what what what do you mean by that?

171
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:58,800
And then how do you, because if you ask this question and people say, you know, okay, I you know, I love your product.

172
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:04,400
I may be terribly disappointed if it, if it goes away, but that might still not tell you why they care.

173
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:12,160
So then how do you dig deeper into those aspects of product market fit to really understand what it is that people care about?

174
00:19:12,560 --> 00:19:15,360
And I love, Ethan, you brought up the jobs to be done framework.

175
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:21,200
I love that job to be done thinking of, of, like, you know, what are they hiring your, your product to do?

176
00:19:21,840 --> 00:19:24,880
And how do you get that out of your, your users?

177
00:19:25,120 --> 00:19:26,720
How do you really understand that from them?

178
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:27,120
Yeah.

179
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:33,520
If, if I could zoom out a little bit on, on that question and then come back to it, but kind of giving, giving that full life cycle.

180
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:40,640
So I think, I think it's not just about, okay, you get to product market fit, you understand it, and then you operationalize growth.

181
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:47,600
Um, I actually think there's, there's sort of more of a more time in between that most startups skip.

182
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:50,640
And Ethan kind of alluded to this that getting to product market.

183
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:55,200
fit is the challenge that causes a lot of companies to go out of business.

184
00:19:56,160 --> 00:19:59,760
And then not understanding their product market fit is another big issue.

185
00:20:00,160 --> 00:20:05,360
I think most of those that don't understand their product market fit still have some level of success.

186
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:09,520
They just they fall well short of what their potential is.

187
00:20:09,760 --> 00:20:13,600
But if they really understand it, they can they can build a great growth engine.

188
00:20:13,760 --> 00:20:22,000
And so really, before you get to the operationalizing of growth, you have to build that initial growth flywheel.

189
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:26,560
And I think that's a pretty different skill set than the operational side.

190
00:20:27,040 --> 00:20:35,680
And so that, that flywheel starts with really understanding who needs it, why do they need it, what's the experience that makes them realize they need it.

191
00:20:35,920 --> 00:20:44,880
So that's usually the first thing that I'm doing when I'm starting on a new business is really just that, the deep insights around that product market fit.

192
00:20:45,520 --> 00:20:48,400
And it's a lot, a lot about just like who and how.

193
00:20:49,040 --> 00:21:00,400
And then, and then trying to build a flywheel of growth about what's the initial check because you get to remember in the beginning you just you don't have a a single channel that's even working.

194
00:21:00,800 --> 00:21:07,680
And so operationalizing and kind of building out dashboards and other things, you sort of have nothing to manage yet at that point.

195
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:11,440
And so really figuring out what is that first channel.

196
00:21:11,600 --> 00:21:14,480
And so before you figure out the channel, you have to figure out who am I reaching?

197
00:21:14,960 --> 00:21:18,560
And so who and then why do they need it?

198
00:21:18,800 --> 00:21:22,960
And then what are the various channels that I could, could, could tap into to reach them?

199
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:25,840
And so it's kind of my channel hypothesis at that point.

200
00:21:26,240 --> 00:21:28,960
And then going through the testing of those channels.

201
00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:36,960
But you also have to think if I'm going to, if I'm going to use this this channel to reach them, what does my business model have to look like?

202
00:21:37,680 --> 00:21:49,200
So if I think I can grow this product virally, then I'm probably not going to have an ultra premium product because the cost is going to really slow down that viral loop.

203
00:21:49,840 --> 00:22:08,400
But if I have to, if I have to pay a lot on the customer acquisition side, let's say I'm going to focus mostly on Google AdWords and it's a pretty competitive set of keywords, then I'm going to need a pretty high price to, to serve, uh, you know, to essentially reach those people and, and pay to convert them as customers.

204
00:22:08,640 --> 00:22:15,040
And so you're not just experimenting with the channels, you're experimenting with the business model that's compatible with those channels.

205
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:18,320
You're experimenting with the onboarding flows.

206
00:22:18,480 --> 00:22:21,840
So at Log Me In, we kind of had everything working, right?

207
00:22:22,080 --> 00:22:38,880
We had the right business model, we had the right channel to get people, but we couldn't make it work in any kind of scalable way until we dialed in that first user experience and really iterated to get new users to a great experience quickly in the product.

208
00:22:40,320 --> 00:22:51,120
Once that was the final piece that essentially said, now this whole thing works and we could we could start building a really scalable engine that that ultimately created a multibillion dollar business.

209
00:22:51,520 --> 00:22:55,200
But yeah, anyway, so you need to get a lot of pieces right first.

210
00:22:55,520 --> 00:23:02,960
And so I think that all the product market fit insights sit at the foundation of getting those pieces right.

211
00:23:05,440 --> 00:23:14,720
And then once you have an initial proven channel that scales, now it starts to look like a real business where you can start to operationalize growth.

212
00:23:15,440 --> 00:23:15,520
Yeah.

213
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:35,040
And then, I mean, Ethan, if you want to chime in, I mean, I'm curious to, from your perspective, like, you know, when you were doing those quick iterations on RoboKiller and other products at Teltek, um, were there questions that you would follow up on to, to understand, to better understand, you know, what that job to be done was?

214
00:23:35,200 --> 00:23:51,360
What, you know, so you're, you know, when you would send out a survey saying, uh, you know, how disappointed would you be if this went away, did you follow up with specific, a specific series of questions to understand why and how and, and, and, you know, tease out some of that understanding of your product market fit?

215
00:23:51,680 --> 00:23:52,240
Yeah, for sure.

216
00:23:52,400 --> 00:24:00,080
I mean, what we wanted to do was really understand at different types of users with different use cases how they were finding value with the product.

217
00:24:00,480 --> 00:24:12,880
So certainly looking at the survey question in not just in a vacuum, like for me, like that's sort of like if you just look at like NPS score, right, it doesn't really tell you what to do, right?

218
00:24:13,920 --> 00:24:22,800
Whereas IOVO is really like Sean's product market fit question, because I think when you contextualize it with other questions, it does become very action.

219
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:28,560
So who are the must have users who said they'd be very disappointed?

220
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:30,880
And then what features do they engage with?

221
00:24:31,120 --> 00:24:32,240
What features do they like?

222
00:24:32,480 --> 00:24:33,200
What don't they like?

223
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:43,520
If you could break it down, segment those users and those audiences, you had a better ability to understand how to make how to improve the product to meet their needs.

224
00:24:43,760 --> 00:24:45,200
So yeah, absolutely.

225
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:49,920
It was a it's a process of using that question contextually with other questions.

226
00:24:50,800 --> 00:24:57,280
And I also think as you zoom out and you start to actually talk to people in the wild, it's not about trying to understand.

227
00:24:57,680 --> 00:24:57,840
Right.

228
00:24:59,440 --> 00:25:08,080
We know certain things from these surveys now that we're talking to other human beings, really trying to understand, are they, do they fit, which categories do they fit in?

229
00:25:08,240 --> 00:25:09,440
How would they describe the product?

230
00:25:09,840 --> 00:25:13,680
What are the challenges they face with the product and how do they kind of fit into that system?

231
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:19,760
So I think, especially with apps, I think it's really easy to not talk to your users.

232
00:25:19,840 --> 00:25:21,280
And we, I was guilty of this.

233
00:25:21,440 --> 00:25:28,000
I would, I would, you know, I'd stand in front of my team and be like, we need to talk to more users, but I had no plan for it at times.

234
00:25:28,240 --> 00:25:31,840
And like, yeah, I, you know, if I could go back in time and and do things over.

235
00:25:32,400 --> 00:25:43,120
Um, that's one of the areas where, uh, I would have, you know, I would have put more effort to try to really come up with a plan for speaking to individuals as individuals, really engaging the community.

236
00:25:43,520 --> 00:25:56,400
You know, we keep hearing, Sean and I keep hearing on our podcast a lot about community led growth, um, and I'm not sure every business is, uh, you know, there's a community to be built or a community is really a a channel for it as a growth channel.

237
00:25:56,880 --> 00:26:01,120
But I think community engaging your community is always important if you want to get more learning out of them.

238
00:26:01,440 --> 00:26:04,240
So yeah, I think there's one, one way to be deliberate about that.

239
00:26:04,640 --> 00:26:14,560
I had a VC who kind of annoyed me in the beginning of log me in, because he just every time I talked to him, he'd say, When was the last time you talked to a customer?

240
00:26:14,640 --> 00:26:23,360
When was it, you know, and I was like, you know, eventually, you know, I think first answer I gave him was kind of a smart assie, you know, I don't really care what customers say.

241
00:26:23,520 --> 00:26:24,160
I care what they do.

242
00:26:24,320 --> 00:26:26,560
And I study the data and I'm, I'm very deep in the data.

243
00:26:26,800 --> 00:26:28,240
And he's like, That's nice.

244
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:29,840
When was the last time you talked to a customer?

245
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:39,520
And so he's, and so, you know, initially I did it just to appease him, but he would just randomly show up in our office and I just wanted to be able to always give him the answer.

246
00:26:39,840 --> 00:26:40,720
Well, today, in fact.

247
00:26:41,120 --> 00:26:43,040
And so, um, so a couple of things.

248
00:26:43,520 --> 00:26:50,160
One, I I started to find I ran much better experiments when I actually talked to customers a lot.

249
00:26:50,320 --> 00:26:53,440
Like, I I just had so much more context into what they were doing.

250
00:26:54,160 --> 00:27:14,800
But the other piece is, and this comes back to the, the plan side that Ethan was just saying, because I kept thinking he might stop in today and I want to be able to answer today, just being deliberate and saying every single day, have at least one customer conversation is super powerful to to stay plugged in and not just think of people as a bunch of numbers.

251
00:27:15,360 --> 00:27:15,440
Wow.

252
00:27:15,760 --> 00:27:16,960
That and that's hard to do.

253
00:27:17,280 --> 00:27:19,120
I think that's why a lot of companies don't do it.

254
00:27:19,280 --> 00:27:21,680
It's it's it's just easier to build in your silo.

255
00:27:21,920 --> 00:27:23,040
It's easier to look at the data.

256
00:27:23,440 --> 00:27:23,520
Yeah.

257
00:27:24,080 --> 00:27:29,200
And, you know, getting on the phone every day with a customer is it's transformational, but it's it's hard.

258
00:27:29,600 --> 00:27:29,680
Yeah.

259
00:27:29,920 --> 00:27:32,320
And it's also which which customers do you talk to?

260
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:40,080
And that's where I think the survey is super powerful in helping to uncover those who say I'd be very disappointed without this, without the product.

261
00:27:40,800 --> 00:27:46,640
But once you once you've uncovered a list of those, like that's a really rich group of people.

262
00:27:50,560 --> 00:27:57,360
And then I love how the next step on the principles of sustainable growth is to quantify improvements with a North Star metric.

263
00:27:57,760 --> 00:28:08,720
So it's not that you don't look at data, it's not that you don't collect data, it's that you contextualize it after starting with the must have experience and really talking with your users.

264
00:28:09,760 --> 00:28:17,920
So I think a lot of people get overwhelmed, myself included, of like what metrics to track, you know, what really should my North Star metric be?

265
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:28,560
So I'd love to have you kind of simpliffied that for me to, you know, and then, you know, Ethan, if you want to talk even more specific to some of your North Star metrics with subscription apps.

266
00:28:29,040 --> 00:28:37,280
Um, but yeah, Sean, tell me about this North Star metric and how to contextualize the data and look for the right data to be actually paying attention to.

267
00:28:37,680 --> 00:28:45,360
Yeah, so I totally agree, like, it's it's super easy to just get a wall of numbers where, where it gets overwhelming.

268
00:28:45,600 --> 00:28:57,120
And so I think the beauty of a North Star metric is that it's it's one metric that rules all other metrics, but it's not, it's not, shouldn't be confused with the North concept of the one metric that matters.

269
00:28:57,360 --> 00:28:59,360
And sometimes people kind of conflate the two.

270
00:29:00,400 --> 00:29:02,000
A lot of metrics matter in the business.

271
00:29:02,560 --> 00:29:02,720
Right.

272
00:29:03,120 --> 00:29:15,040
But the beauty of a North Star metric is that it reflects what you're trying to do with the North Star metric is reflect how much aggregate value are I actually delivering to my customers.

273
00:29:15,440 --> 00:29:24,800
So you once you understand what the must have experience is with the product, how many units of that experience am I delivering?

274
00:29:25,120 --> 00:29:27,680
And so you might say, like, why that instead of just revenue?

275
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,400
Like revenue is where it all hangs off.

276
00:29:30,960 --> 00:29:43,280
But if you if you just focus on revenue and and value is not moving at the same pace as revenue, then eventually revenue is going to crash and you're going to be disappointed.

277
00:29:43,440 --> 00:29:55,760
But if you can focus on how do I keep growing that footprint of value and have revenue be a function of that value, then revenue is going to be much more sustainable.

278
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:59,280
And so figuring it out then becomes it becomes a challenge.

279
00:29:59,920 --> 00:30:36,000
But the way that I tend to do it is I go through an explanation, I give some examples of companies, like Facebook being daily active users, but Slack is also daily active users, so it's not just a B to C metric, and then Uber would be weekly rides, and so once people hear a few examples, they start to, they start to kind of understand the concept better, and then I have essentially in a workshop get everyone to come up with their own ideas for the North Star metric, and what I tell them is you can debate it for six months.

280
00:30:36,720 --> 00:30:50,400
And then you don't get the power of the North Star metric and it's probably not going to be any better than if we spend thirty minutes or an hour on this and at the end of the hour we're going to commit to something and if we, you know, in a few months need to change it, so be it.

281
00:30:50,560 --> 00:30:52,800
We change it, but like, let's commit to something.

282
00:30:53,200 --> 00:31:02,640
And so, um, but it really starts with what is the value that people get from the product and how do I make sure that the metric So you have to be able to track the metric.

283
00:31:02,720 --> 00:31:03,920
That's one, one thing.

284
00:31:04,640 --> 00:31:08,160
So a lot of metrics like, oh, that would be a perfect metric.

285
00:31:08,560 --> 00:31:13,040
So particularly in marketplace types businesses where they may not be actually tracking the transaction.

286
00:31:13,280 --> 00:31:15,120
They're more of just matching people up.

287
00:31:16,400 --> 00:31:20,960
I've seen people have a, have a hard time coming up with a metric that really works well there.

288
00:31:21,600 --> 00:31:31,120
But if you can, if you like in the case of Uber that we talked about, every time there is a ride delivered on Uber, someone gets value.

289
00:31:31,840 --> 00:31:36,880
The rider got where they needed to go and a driver made some money taking them there.

290
00:31:37,440 --> 00:31:44,720
And so if I just track how many rides I'm delivering in aggregate across the system in, in, on a weekly.

291
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:48,640
basis, so that kind of normalizes for differences on different days of the week.

292
00:31:49,360 --> 00:31:55,440
And I know that's going up every single week, then my footprint of value is growing a lot.

293
00:31:55,600 --> 00:32:06,160
And, and a lot of times people get kind of caught up in, yeah, but if it's a drive to Las Vegas versus a drive to the grocery store around the corner, like you're not properly representing that.

294
00:32:06,480 --> 00:32:10,560
And, and I think it comes back to what Ethan was saying in the jobs to be done.

295
00:32:11,600 --> 00:32:13,280
The job is to get where you need to go.

296
00:32:13,760 --> 00:32:18,560
And so every time you help someone achieve their goal, you're not just.

297
00:32:19,040 --> 00:32:24,880
providing value, but you're also building a habit around we are the product that helps you achieve that goal.

298
00:32:25,280 --> 00:32:30,480
And so long term, one ride to Las Vegas might be more money for the business.

299
00:32:31,520 --> 00:32:47,200
But if I could have 100 rides that add up to the same cost as that one ride, that's much more valuable because 100 rides created a habit of now, whenever I need to get somewhere, Uber comes to mind first as the way I get there.

300
00:32:47,760 --> 00:32:47,840
Yeah.

301
00:32:48,160 --> 00:32:49,360
That's such a fantastic example.

302
00:32:49,680 --> 00:32:49,760
Yeah.

303
00:32:49,920 --> 00:32:52,640
And then I was going to ask you, Thane, any specific.

304
00:32:53,280 --> 00:33:00,560
examples related to your work at Teltek or just industry, you know, subscription app specific kind of North Star metrics you think about?

305
00:33:00,800 --> 00:33:04,800
Yeah, just going back to what Sean said, I think one of the key things is just keeping it simple.

306
00:33:05,120 --> 00:33:16,400
I always say, like, if I'm trying to train a company on growth and we're talking about North Star metric, I'm like, if it's an algorithm and it comes out to 0.61, right, it's not going to, like, it's too complicated, right?

307
00:33:17,600 --> 00:33:23,840
In the telecom business, a lot of times, like, calls handled often would be a good metric, but, you know, that doesn't work for every business.

308
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:31,280
And you could have two companies competing in the same space with two different North Star metrics because it's really about the shared understanding amongst the team.

309
00:33:31,600 --> 00:33:41,360
And I think that's where, and we learned this with Sean through his workshop and, you know, something I really believe in, like, it's that shared understanding of the North Star metric that's important.

310
00:33:41,600 --> 00:33:50,160
It's everyone looking at, you know, it really breaks down silos when everyone's looking across the organization and say, what really matters to end users?

311
00:33:50,560 --> 00:33:52,800
How is, how do we quantify value, right?

312
00:33:53,120 --> 00:33:58,720
Because ultimately, if what you're doing isn't impacting value for end users, you have to ask yourself, why are we doing it?

313
00:33:59,360 --> 00:34:04,800
So, you know, I think it's an incredibly powerful tool, but I think it's easy to complicate.

314
00:34:05,760 --> 00:34:10,480
Sean and I often hear companies say, we ask them what their North Star metric and they say, well, we have a bunch of them.

315
00:34:10,560 --> 00:34:12,720
And I'm like, well, there's only one North Star in the sky.

316
00:34:13,680 --> 00:34:16,480
There are cases where it makes sense to have more than one, but I think people right.

317
00:34:16,960 --> 00:34:18,080
People right.

318
00:34:18,480 --> 00:34:21,520
People go right down to some of the KPIs thinking those are North Star metrics.

319
00:34:21,680 --> 00:34:27,840
And again, what to, you know, if people get anything from this conversation, it's not saying you only need one metric.

320
00:34:28,240 --> 00:34:33,280
It's saying a North Star metric helps align teams, get everyone pointed in the same direction around value.

321
00:34:34,320 --> 00:34:37,920
But there are many other metrics that will matter that ladder up to that.

322
00:34:38,160 --> 00:34:46,800
And I think one of the key things I look at when coming up with a North Star metric, I try to walk through like retention, referral, acquisition, activation.

323
00:34:46,960 --> 00:34:52,400
And I try to ask myself, how would anything in this growth engine impact that North Star metric?

324
00:34:52,480 --> 00:35:03,680
Because if I can't really tie, like how more people coming into the app or more people staying longer or telling their friends about it would impact that metric, it's probably not the right metric for me.

325
00:35:04,720 --> 00:35:13,120
But I do think keeping it simple and understanding that you may have to adapt, evolve it over time as, you know, keys to being successful with the North Star metric.

326
00:35:13,440 --> 00:35:13,520
Yeah.

327
00:35:13,840 --> 00:35:19,280
And then the next thing on your principles is diagram your value delivery engine.

328
00:35:19,680 --> 00:35:21,520
I imagine that exercise.

329
00:35:22,400 --> 00:35:31,280
Um, actually might even help you better understand that what really should be your North Star metric and just better help you understand your business.

330
00:35:31,840 --> 00:35:36,000
So what do you mean, uh, Sean, by, by diagramming your value delivery engine?

331
00:35:36,160 --> 00:35:37,600
Like what, how do you do that?

332
00:35:37,920 --> 00:35:39,760
So Ethan actually just touched on it right there.

333
00:35:39,920 --> 00:35:48,000
It's, it's really thinking about if you start with your North Star metricric and you just start to think, what are the different ways we could move this?

334
00:35:48,400 --> 00:35:55,600
And traditionally, people just think, okay, we're more effective on customer acquisition and that's going to move the North Star metric.

335
00:35:56,080 --> 00:35:58,800
We get more people in the door and that's going to move it.

336
00:35:59,200 --> 00:36:08,480
But if the majority of the people you get in the door never actually experience the product, then they then they contribute nothing to that North Star metric.

337
00:36:08,560 --> 00:36:10,080
So they got no value.

338
00:36:10,320 --> 00:36:16,800
So then it becomes, okay, activation, how do I actually get them to experience that value the first time?

339
00:36:18,240 --> 00:36:19,520
is a big part of that engine.

340
00:36:19,760 --> 00:36:30,960
So, so, and then with the with the recognition that if it takes them, you know, months and months to get to that first experience of value, most people are going to give up on that before, before they ever experience it.

341
00:36:31,200 --> 00:36:34,960
And so speed to value is part of that, that, that kind of equation there.

342
00:36:35,600 --> 00:36:37,760
But, um, but there's a lot of ways to move.

343
00:36:37,840 --> 00:36:40,240
I can get my existing users to bring in additional users.

344
00:36:40,560 --> 00:36:52,000
So my, my referral engine on the business, even revenue loop to some degree, while revenue may not grow the North Star metric directly, it funds the program.s that grow the metric.

345
00:36:52,160 --> 00:36:58,800
So it's kind of it's more of a dependent variable on acquisition, for example, or even activation.

346
00:36:59,040 --> 00:37:07,440
If I've got an incentive to drive activation or referral, having more money that's generated from my customers is going to help that.

347
00:37:07,600 --> 00:37:09,520
And then engagement is obviously a huge one as well.

348
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:22,240
If I can get people to experience the product more often for longer times each time they experience it, that's going to, you know, one person can contribute a lot more to the North Star metric.

349
00:37:22,560 --> 00:37:46,560
But I think the real key here is just as eas Ethan was saying earlier, it's not just quantifying value and knowing that revenue sustainability is going to be tied to value delivery sustainability, but it's also now you start to get towards that operationalizing of growth where one of the biggest detriments to growth that I see in a business is that silo that just happened naturally.

350
00:37:46,880 --> 00:37:48,960
As you grow, you get more specialized skills.

351
00:37:49,920 --> 00:37:58,720
I've seen a sales culture be stronger than the company culture or customer support culture be stronger and have almost no communication.

352
00:37:59,680 --> 00:38:01,280
or context with other groups.

353
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:06,880
And then, and then I have my metric that's important, which might be engagement on customers.

354
00:38:07,120 --> 00:38:29,200
If I'm saying on the customer support side, and then I get upset when the, when the marketing team brings in lower engaged customers, even though maybe those lower engaged customers are hugely profitable for us and help to move the North Star metric in aggregate, but just on average, they don't help as much, which hurt this other group's metric.

355
00:38:29,520 --> 00:38:37,920
So if everyone can zoom out and say, okay., this is the metric I'm focused on, but the most important metric is the North Star metric.

356
00:38:38,320 --> 00:38:48,640
And so if I'm, if I'm having a strong conversation with another group that maybe hurts my metric, the tiebreaker is, but does this help the North Star metric?

357
00:38:49,120 --> 00:38:50,400
And it really starts.

358
00:38:50,960 --> 00:39:04,000
And then what's kind of cool with that is that you have this, like, common metric that starts to matter, but you also have, you know, the North Star metric tends to map a lot more to the, the mission and vision of the business as well.

359
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:06,720
And so I think another problem is that.

360
00:39:06,720 --> 00:39:17,360
Another problem with the siloed world is that people lack context on the big picture and their job starts to suck if they don't have context on the big picture.

361
00:39:17,520 --> 00:39:32,960
And so if you can reinforce mission and how the North Star metric is progress against that mission, and this is my role in moving the North Star metric and helping us progress against that mission, there's just a lot more meaning in everyday work for people as well.

362
00:39:33,440 --> 00:39:34,800
Yeah, I would echo that.

363
00:39:34,960 --> 00:39:45,920
And I think where, you know, just it can be eye opening just to look at your growth engine as this diagram and start looking at how these pieces interact with each other and how they do eventually move the North Star metric.

364
00:39:46,320 --> 00:40:04,880
And I do think that, you know, I mean, a good old example was when we were at uproar dot com, I drew this, uh, this banner ad and, uh, Sean will get a kick out of it because we were, I was trying to beat our existing mix of banner ads every week and I finally was bored but I couldn't think of anything really one day.

365
00:40:05,040 --> 00:40:12,720
And I put my finger on a piece of paper, I drew an outline around it and I came up with the pull my finger banner and right away, like, we were like amazed.

366
00:40:13,200 --> 00:40:14,800
With a little audio symbol on there.

367
00:40:14,880 --> 00:40:17,040
there that you're going to hear something if you pull the finger.

368
00:40:18,160 --> 00:40:19,920
I'm not really proud of it, but it was funny.

369
00:40:21,600 --> 00:40:44,400
But yeah, like when we when we came up with that at first when we saw the, you know, people were just clicking on it like crazy and I got so excited about it later on, you know, we started to realize that it wasn't really reflective of the value we were creating and it didn't, you know, it didn't have the I mean, it had such a high click rate that it, it made up for it, but the conversion rate wasn't very, wasn't really that good.

370
00:40:44,960 --> 00:40:50,960
And I think I realized that all these things work together.

371
00:40:51,280 --> 00:40:58,800
If you don't understand your product market fit and how to dial it in, then the best growth engine in the world is probably not going to be super useful.

372
00:40:59,200 --> 00:41:01,760
Having that North Star metric to tie everything together.

373
00:41:02,000 --> 00:41:08,240
I mean, I think if I could have seen, like, okay, well, I can bring these type of people in, they're not necessarily our regular users.

374
00:41:08,400 --> 00:41:12,000
What, you know, what games in our portfolio might appeal to them.

375
00:41:12,400 --> 00:41:14,160
But, yeah, I was pretty young at the time.

376
00:41:14,720 --> 00:41:18,080
One thing just, just to build off that real fast, because I think that's a really important.

377
00:41:18,560 --> 00:41:20,560
So we didn't call it a North Star metric back then.

378
00:41:20,720 --> 00:41:20,720
Yeah.

379
00:41:20,960 --> 00:41:22,800
Like, I don't think that, that term I used.

380
00:41:22,960 --> 00:41:26,400
I didn't even become familiar with it until like within the last ten years.

381
00:41:27,200 --> 00:41:34,480
But what we did know was that value was represented in the amount of time people spent playing games on the site.

382
00:41:35,200 --> 00:41:48,080
And because we optimized everything for that, so we took that high click through rate on that banner that Ethan created and then ultimately said, what is the cost per unit of engagement in our product?

383
00:41:48,640 --> 00:41:53,920
And then that one actually made a pretty good cut, even though the average engagement of those people was lower.

384
00:41:56,000 --> 00:42:10,000
And if you zoom out and look at that business, we had the highest average engagement of any company on the planet at a time when most people were, you know, they consider it sophisticated to go beyond CPM tracking to CPC tracking.

385
00:42:11,200 --> 00:42:21,200
We were literally down to every dollar spent here, how many hours of engagement or minutes of engagement were we creating per dollar spent in our campaigns.

386
00:42:21,360 --> 00:42:30,960
And so that required really sophisticated tracking, but I think it got everyone in the company on the same page and got it to work.

